// Gangster: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== gangster.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Queen Of The Forty
genre: gangster + crime
tone: Brassy, quick-fingered and unsentimental
era: 1921 | period
setting: The West End shops and the terraces south of the river, London | real | city
logline: The six-foot queen of an all-women gang that has been stripping London's department stores for a generation takes on a clever new girl, and has to decide whether she is training a successor or a rival.
premise: The men's gangs south of the river fight over racecourses. The women's gang works the West End in fur coats with hidden pockets, drives to the job in fast cars, and pays wages when a member is inside. Its rules are strict: no drinking before a job, no wearing what you steal, no men's opinions. {maggie} has led it for six years with diamond rings on every finger, which do service as a knuckleduster.
synopsis: The first act is a day's work filmed as choreography: six women enter a store by six doors; a counter of silk is emptied into bloomers built for the purpose; a decoy faints; a car is waiting. {maggie} takes a cut, pays the fines, runs a party that night at which nothing stolen is worn. A girl of nineteen, {lil}, a parlourmaid with references she wrote herself, asks to join and is put through a trial that is half audition, half hazing. She is very good. The second act widens the trade and the trouble. {lil} proposes placing maids in big houses with forged characters and clearing them at leisure; the takings double. She also falls for a bookmaker's runner from the men's gang, which is against the rules, since men gossip and bring police. A detective-sergeant, {capper}, who has arrested {maggie} eleven times and rather admires her, begins to get tips. The older hand {old_poll}, queen before {maggie}, says a gang is a family until the day it is a business, and then somebody gets hurt. When {lil} announces she will marry, {maggie} goes to the wedding party with a dozen women and bricks, and it becomes a riot that reaches the newspapers. In the third act {maggie} stands trial for the riot. The magistrate would like a moral. {lil}, in the gallery, has the gang's book now and could run it. {maggie} gets eighteen months and blows a kiss to the court. In the cells {old_poll} visits and tells her that every queen is certain the next one is a traitor, and is always right, and that it does not matter. The last scene is a West End store at opening time: six women, six doors, {lil} in the fur coat, and a store detective reading a wanted notice with an old photograph.
question: Can you hold power in a family without turning it into a firm?
themes: women's work outside the law; rules as love; succession
audience: Adults who like crime with style and no lecture
viewpoint: Split between {maggie} and {lil}, never the police
stakes: A gang's unity, a queen's freedom, and a girl's choice between two kinds of belonging
conflict: A leader's code against a protégée's ambition and heart, with the law waiting for the split
ending: The queen goes down for the riot. The girl takes the coat. The trade goes on.
experience: The glee of a perfect lift, a party that turns, and a hard little shrug at the end
look: Plate glass, fur and electric light; soot-brick terraces; rings catching the light on a raised fist
sound: Shop bells, a getaway engine, ragtime from a gramophone. The clink of rings set down on a police desk.
m: keep | rival | loyalty-vs-truth | ironic
@ maggie | Maggie Hale | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 34 | The gang whole and herself at the head | It is the only kingdom a girl from her street was ever offered | She has trained the one who will take it | Grand, funny, violent when crossed, generous by rule | From absolute queen to a prisoner blowing kisses | Loud, south-London, royal plural when pleased | Six foot, a fur coat, diamonds on every finger
@ lil | Lil Dacre | co-lead | rival | human | she/her | 19 | A life bigger than service | She has scrubbed other people's steps since twelve | She wants the gang and a man, and the rules say choose | Quick, cool, a planner | From trial girl to queen | Soft maid's voice she can drop like a tray | Neat black, white cuffs, later the coat
@ old_poll | Old Poll | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 62 | To see it last | She was queen once and was pushed | Nobody listens to a retired thief | Wry, unshockable | From chorus to the one who says the true thing | Slow, amused | Shawl, a stout stick, a gin she nurses
@ capper | Detective-Sergeant Capper | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 45 | One conviction that sticks | Eleven arrests and she waves at him in the street | He likes her | Patient, fair, dogged | From pursuer to a man reading the next name | Dry, courteous | Bowler, a notebook, flat feet
~ maggie - lil | queen and recruit | a trial set and passed | a coat changing shoulders
~ maggie - old_poll | queen and former queen | an old woman kept on wages | a warning that comes true
~ maggie - capper | thief and detective | a game with rules | eighteen months and a kiss blown
